  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nIR currently depicts mainly clear skies for the northern half of\nthe CWA with mid and upper level clouds spilling in from the\nsouth for southern portions of the state from a weak trough\nacross northern Colorado. WV shows the main finger of the PFJ to\nthe north dipping down from the Gulf of Alaska to the Pacific\nNorthwest around the next main L/W trough that will affect the\nupper Rockies by Sunday. For Friday, expect a bit warmer than\naverage temperatures and gusty winds due to an increasing\npressure gradient ahead of an approaching west to east moving\ncold front Friday night into Saturday morning. Gusty winds 15 to\n25 mph will be common for much of the area in the afternoon\nduring peak heating and mixing, stronger for the Wind Corridor \nfrom Sweetwater to Natrona Counties where gusts could reach 30 \nto 35 mph. Winds look to weaken but remain elevated after sunset\nas the aforementioned cold front starts to push in out west \nbefore stalling along the Divide by sunrise Saturday morning. A \nlittle bit of model disagreement on how far it reaches, but \noverall, dry conditions will be expected. With the weak trough \nto the south, expect some isolated rain showers and \nthunderstorms to push across far eastern reaches of Sweetwater\nCounty through Muddy Gap to Casper early to late afternoon with\nminimal accumulations and/or impacts expected. Otherwise, \nincreased clouds throughout the CWA in the mid and upper levels \nahead of the deepening trough to the west with increased \ndivergence aloft ahead of it. \n\nMore seasonable temperatures west of the Divide Saturday with a\nbit above average to the east, dependent on how far east the\ncold front pushes. Gusty winds continue Saturday as well similar\nto Friday. As the main low skirts to the north across southern\nCanada late Saturday into Sunday, the southern end of the trough\nbegins to cut off near California. This will deepen as well with\nthe increased upper level divergence as it progresses slowly\neastward bringing the more conducive southwest flow across the\nCWA. Expect scattered shower activity west of the Divide late\nSunday morning, expanding to the east later Sunday evening and\ninto the overnight hours and into Monday morning. The low pushes\neast, across the Southern Rockies and into the High Plains by \nTuesday that will end precipitation chances. Accumulations at \nthis time don't look overwhelming, but all areas have a decent \nchance for a wetting rain (>.1\") around 50 to 90% in coverage, \nand again best chances west of the Divide. Mountain snow is \nlikely as well, as temperatures become more seasonable and \nfreezing levels drop to the 10-11kft range. An inch or two is \npossible in the higher elevations of the Winds (mainly the \nwestern side), along with the Absarokas and northern Bighorns. \nAny mountain passes should stay void of any snowfall but there \nis time for change being a few days out. Stay tuned for updated \nforecasts in the coming day or two for the models to come into \nbetter agreement on the movement of the cut-off low from \nCalifornia through the southern Rockies.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, expect upper level ridging to build back in from\nthe southwest for the rest of the week and into the weekend.\nTemperatures will moderate back to seasonable Wednesday onward\nwith dry conditions expected there on. The next system looks to\nimpact the CWA and Northern Rockies heading into the following \nweekend looking at the longer term model solutions but time will\ntell.",
